WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.758
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.058 --> 00:00:08.328
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.628 --> 00:00:15.960
Unit twenty-five: Data Governance. Today you will reason about risk using if + were to (formal hypothesis).

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.842
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.126
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:25.217
Data governance.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.608
Data governance means the rules for how data is collected, stored and used.

8
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:34.458
Data governance defines who may access patient records.

9
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.876
Your turn. Say: Data governance.

10
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.013
Data governance.

11
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:43.844
Privacy.

12
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.336
Privacy means the right to keep personal information protected.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.251
Privacy is a basic condition of freedom.

14
00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.166
Your turn. Say: Privacy.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:57.943
Privacy.

16
00:00:58.683 --> 00:00:59.603
Consent.

17
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:03.266
Consent means voluntary and informed agreement.

18
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.312
Consent must be clear, not buried in small print.

19
00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:10.023
Your turn. Say: Consent.

20
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:13.833
Consent.

21
00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:15.747
Regulation.

22
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.597
Regulation means official rules governing conduct.

23
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:22.996
Regulation requires firms to report breaches.

24
00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:26.085
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

25
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:30.190
Regulation.

26
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.951
Ownership.

27
00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.785
Ownership means the legal right to control something.

28
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:38.836
The ownership of personal data is disputed.

29
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.300
Your turn. Say: Ownership.

30
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:46.053
Ownership.

31
00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:47.992
Security.

32
00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:52.388
Security means protection against unauthorised access.

33
00:01:52.983 --> 00:01:55.041
Weak security allowed the breach.

34
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.244
Your turn. Say: Security.

35
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.264
Security.

36
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:04.389
Accountability.

37
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:09.148
Accountability means the obligation to answer for one's actions.

38
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.644
Accountability means firms must report leaks.

39
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.053
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

40
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.203
Accountability.

41
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:21.719
Trust.

42
00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:26.365
Trust means confidence in the reliability of a person or system.

43
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.495
Trust is easy to lose and hard to rebuild.

44
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:32.306
Your turn. Say: Trust.

45
00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:35.936
Trust.

46
00:02:36.733 --> 00:02:40.338
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:47.052
Visal, apps ask for my location, contacts and photos. What is the worst that could happen?

48
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:55.862
If data were to leak, trust would collapse, and individuals could be exposed to fraud or harassment.

49
00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.624
Would the company be punished?

50
00:02:59.717 --> 00:03:08.046
Under strong regulation, yes. If a firm were to hide a breach, it would face heavy fines, and executives could be held accountable.

51
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.499
And who owns the data?

52
00:03:11.583 --> 00:03:22.386
That is disputed. If individuals were to own their data, they could demand payment or deletion, but firms would argue that processing creates value.

53
00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:25.196
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.872
Grammar focus: If plus were to (formal hypothesis).

55
00:03:30.172 --> 00:03:31.406
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.117 --> 00:03:34.615
If data were to leak, trust would collapse.

57
00:03:35.517 --> 00:03:38.944
If a firm were to hide a breach, it would face heavy fines.

58
00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:43.440
If individuals were to own their data, they could demand deletion.

59
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:55.795
If + subject + were to + base verb imagines an unlikely or formal event: If data were to leak, trust would collapse. Use 'were' for all subjects; the result clause takes would/could.

60
00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.445
Be careful! Don't say: If data would leak, trust collapses.

61
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.235
Say: If data were to leak, trust would collapse.

62
00:04:05.535 --> 00:04:07.059
If + were to … would.

63
00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:10.310
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.380
Who Owns Your Data?.

65
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.826
Digital services run on personal data, from our location to our habits and relationships.

66
00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:25.883
Data governance sets rules for collection, storage and use, and it rests on privacy, informed consent and accountability.

67
00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:32.883
If data were to leak, trust would collapse, and individuals might suffer fraud, discrimination or harassment.

68
00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:39.558
If regulators were to demand transparency and heavy penalties, firms would invest more in security.

69
00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:46.943
Whether data should be owned like property, or protected like a right, remains one of the central questions of the digital age.

70
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:52.047
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.883 --> 00:04:55.545
If data blank to leak, trust would collapse.

72
00:04:55.845 --> 00:04:59.196
A, were. B, was. C, is.

73
00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:04.605
The answer is A: were.

74
00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.365
Voluntary and informed agreement is blank.

75
00:05:08.665 --> 00:05:12.539
A, consent. B, ownership. C, security.

76
00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.960
The answer is A: consent.

77
00:05:19.117 --> 00:05:22.033
The obligation to answer for one's actions is blank.

78
00:05:22.333 --> 00:05:26.103
A, privacy. B, accountability. C, trust.

79
00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:31.945
The answer is B: accountability.

80
00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:34.700
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:43.866
A, If firms were to hide leaks, they would be fined.. B, If firms was to hide leaks, they will be fined.. C, If firms were hide leaks, they would fined..

82
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.153
The answer is A: If firms were to hide leaks, they would be fined..

83
00:05:52.317 --> 00:05:56.464
According to the reading, strong penalties would make firms invest more in…

84
00:05:56.764 --> 00:06:01.532
A, advertising. B, security. C, ownership.

85
00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:06.997
The answer is B: security.

86
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.363
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:12.217 --> 00:06:14.518
Who should own the data people create online?

88
00:06:31.317 --> 00:06:32.782
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.082 --> 00:06:55.721
I think individuals should have strong control over the data they create, even if companies hold it for specific services. People should know what is collected, be able to correct or delete it, and decide whether it can be shared. If firms were to own it outright, the incentive to protect privacy would weaken. Control and accountability protect trust.

90
00:06:56.021 --> 00:06:57.695
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:58.583 --> 00:07:03.755
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and if plus were to (formal hypothesis).

92
00:07:04.055 --> 00:07:08.251
Today's value is digital trust. Protect the trust that others place in you.

93
00:07:08.551 --> 00:07:11.622
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:11.922 --> 00:07:14.888
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

